// A module's first file with no 'mod' line. Every file the compiler builds as a
// module opens with one [Jon 24 Sep]: a lone file like this one, a module's
// root, a folder module's designated file and a one-file submodule. Its name is
// the file's, or the folder's, so the line restates what the filesystem says
// where a reader of the file looks first. Its first statement is refused, and
// the file is still parsed as written, as the module its file names.
//
// Parse stage, which is why it is its own scenario. The imported modules are
// modulenomod.cone, which opens with a module trait -- no 'mod' line either --
// and modulecomments.cone, which holds only comments; each is refused the same
// way, in its own file. A submodule's designated file with none is in
// module_submodule_reject (bare/).

import modulenomod;     //~ ErrorNoModDcl:1 "begin it with 'mod module_parse_nomod;'"
import modulecomments;

fn nomod() i64 {
  1i64;
}
